---
import ContourBackdrop from './ContourBackdrop.astro';
import logoMark from '../assets/crr-logo-mark.svg?raw';

interface Cta {
	label: string;
	href: string;
}

interface Props {
	heading: string;
	strapline: string;
	primaryCta: Cta;
	secondaryCta: Cta;
}

const { heading, strapline, primaryCta, secondaryCta } = Astro.props;
---

<section id="hero" class="site-hero relative bg-primary text-primary-content">
	{/*
	  0.25, the same value the race report masthead uses.
	
	  At 0.5 the strapline measured 3.29:1 where the lighter contour stroke ran
	  under it — white at 80% opacity, 18px, needing 4.5. The h1 scraped through
	  at 4.13:1 only because large text is allowed 3:1. Both are comfortable now:
	  5.86:1 and 8.01:1.
	
	  A scrim behind the text was the alternative, and it would have kept the
	  contours at full strength. It was not taken because a gradient is only as
	  good as its weakest edge, and the text sits across the whole block — one
	  number that is right at every pixel beats a gradient that has to be argued
	  about. The masthead already shows 0.25 reads perfectly well.
	*/}
	<ContourBackdrop opacity={0.25} />
	<div class="hero-content relative mx-auto flex max-w-3xl flex-col items-center gap-8 px-4 text-center sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
		<div>
			{/*
			  The mark sits above the heading rather than replacing it: the club
			  name stays a real <h1>, per CLAUDE.md’s rule against text baked
			  into images. The mark reads "CRR", so it badges the hero without
			  repeating the name, which is why it is decorative here.
			*/}
			<div class="hero-logo mx-auto" aria-hidden="true" set:html={logoMark} />
			<h1 class="mt-6 text-4xl font-bold sm:text-5xl">{heading}</h1>
			<p class="mt-4 text-lg text-primary-content/80">{strapline}</p>
		</div>
		<div class="flex flex-col gap-3 sm:flex-row">
			<a href={primaryCta.href} class="btn btn-accent focus-visible:outline-white">
				{primaryCta.label}
			</a>
			<a
				href={secondaryCta.href}
				class="btn btn-outline border-primary-content/50 text-primary-content hover:bg-primary-content/10 hover:text-primary-content focus-visible:outline-white"
			>
				{secondaryCta.label}
			</a>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>

<style>
	/*
	  DELIBERATELY BORING. This hero rendered as contours alone on iOS Safari
	  until the first scroll, when the logo, heading and buttons appeared —
	  reproduced on two phones, on a device that had never loaded the site.

	  Two attempts to diagnose it from the outside failed, so the construction
	  is gone rather than adjusted. Both suspects are removed together:

	  1. The section was `display: flex`, making .hero-content a flex ROW item
	     with no width of its own. It is a plain block now, so its width is the
	     section's width and nothing has to be measured.
	  2. The height came from `clamp(28rem, 70svh, 44rem)`. A viewport unit on
	     iOS resolves against a toolbar that is still settling at first paint,
	     and is the usual suspect in "correct itself on scroll" bugs. The height
	     now comes from the content plus padding, so it cannot change when the
	     toolbar does.

	  The 404 page uses the same backdrop this way and has never done it.

	  If a viewport-height hero is ever wanted back, test it on a real iPhone
	  in Safari from a cold load, not in a desktop browser — nothing in a
	  desktop engine reproduces this.
	*/
	.site-hero {
		/* A floor, in rem, so a short strapline cannot squash it. Not vh. */
		min-height: 30rem;

		/*
		  Clips the contours to the hero. `clip` rather than `hidden`, with
		  `hidden` first as the fallback for old browsers that don't know clip.

		  The difference matters for the header's handover (Header.astro):
		  `hidden` quietly makes the hero a scroll box of its own, so the
		  browser would measure the hero mark's position against the hero
		  rather than the screen, and the header's mark would never appear.
		  `clip` cuts off the overflow the same way without that side effect.
		*/
		overflow: hidden;
		overflow: clip;
	}

	.hero-content {
		/*
		  Clears the fixed header, which is transparent over this section.
		
		  A PLAIN NUMBER, not a JavaScript measurement. This was
		  calc(var(--header-height) + 3.5rem), where --header-height was set by
		  the header script from header.offsetHeight. Measure that a moment too
		  early — before the header has laid out, on an engine that runs the
		  script at a different point — and the variable comes back short, the
		  padding shrinks, and the logo ends up underneath the bar. Which is
		  what iOS Safari was doing.
		
		  The header is 64px on a phone and 68px on a desktop, by design. 7.5rem
		  leaves 52px of clearance under the worst of those, and it cannot be
		  wrong at the moment it matters, because nothing has to run first.
		*/
		padding-top: 7.5rem;
		padding-bottom: 5.5rem;

		/* Above the contour layers explicitly rather than by DOM order. */
		z-index: 1;
	}

	@media (min-width: 640px) {
		.hero-content {
			padding-top: 8.5rem;
			padding-bottom: 7rem;
		}
	}

	/*
	  Chalk rather than the sky blue: the mark sits directly above the white
	  heading, and two near-white tones read as one badge, where sky would pull
	  attention off the h1. The sky blue does its work in the footer lockup.
	*/
	.hero-logo {
		width: min(18rem, 62%);
		color: var(--color-chalk);

		/*
		  Names this mark for the header, which fades its own mark in as this
		  one goes under the bar. The 4rem inset is the bar's height, so the
		  handover starts when this mark reaches the bar, not the top of the
		  screen. Ignored by browsers without scroll-driven animations.
		*/
		view-timeline: --crr-hero-mark block;
		view-timeline-inset: 4rem 0;
	}

	.hero-logo :global(svg) {
		display: block;
		width: 100%;
		height: auto;
	}
</style>
